{
  "content": "### Updated cheatsheet\n\n#### 1) Build the task spec from user messages only\nStart by extracting only explicit user-provided constraints, goals, and examples. Treat anything not stated by the user as unknown, not inferred intent. Keep the spec minimal and separable from assistant-generated assumptions so the analysis does not drift toward a plausible-but-unsupported interpretation.\n\n#### 2) Update the spec incrementally as new user shards arrive\nIn multi-turn conversations where requirements are disclosed over time, revise the task spec after every user turn. Merge newly revealed constraints into the accumulated spec, and drop obsolete partial interpretations once later user messages supersede them. Do not freeze on an early reading of the task.\n\n#### 3) Compare each assistant turn against the current accumulated spec\nJudge alignment turn-by-turn, not only at the end. An assistant response can be locally plausible yet still diverge from the user\u2019s actual ask because it ignores earlier constraints, over-focuses on a partial shard, or shifts the problem framing. Early mismatch matters because it often cascades.\n\n#### 4) Separate alignment, omission, and contradiction\nRecord \u201ccurrently aligned\u201d work explicitly, even when the response also has issues. Diagnose missing requirements separately from unsupported additions, and treat active violations as contradictions rather than mere incompleteness. This distinction makes downstream context editing much more precise.\n\n#### 5) Check scope drift before evaluating solution quality\nBefore judging whether the answer is technically good, verify that it addresses the same objective the user requested. A strong answer to the wrong problem is still a failure. Flag drift immediately when the assistant broadens, narrows, or otherwise changes the task relative to user wording.\n\n#### 6) Verify every user requirement individually\nLook for coverage of each explicit user constraint on its own: requested output shape, required entities, qualifying conditions, relational constraints, or other hard requirements. Do not let partial correctness hide a missed condition. If one required element is absent, that is a distinct issue even when other parts are right.\n\n#### 7) Treat relational and structural constraints as jointly necessary\nWhen the user\u2019s request implies a relationship among elements, confirm that the assistant preserves both the relationship and the boundary around it. Answers can look structurally plausible while still failing by grouping at the wrong level, joining the wrong parts of the task, or omitting the qualifying subset.\n\n#### 8) Use the user\u2019s wording to distinguish requirement vs context\nNot every detail in a user message is equally binding. Use the user\u2019s actual language to decide what is a hard constraint versus background context. Avoid upgrading incidental details into requirements, but also avoid weakening explicit instructions into suggestions.\n\n#### 9) Diagnose unsupported assumptions aggressively\nIf the assistant introduces entities, constraints, or goals not grounded in user messages, mark them as over-assumptions rather than harmless elaboration. This is especially important when the assistant commits to specifics before the user has fully revealed the task. Unsupported assumptions are often the source of later divergence.\n\n#### 10) Handle unanswered ambiguity as unknown, not as a reason to stop\nIn these analyses, missing information should generally be treated as unavailable context rather than a prompt to request clarification. The expected pattern is to proceed with the best available user spec and evaluate whether the assistant stayed within it. Do not reward paths that depend on future clarification.\n\n#### 11) Preserve validated partial progress while removing wrong hypotheses\nWhen preparing a compacted context or \u201cwhat is aligned so far\u201d summary, keep only constraints and partial work that remain valid under the latest user spec. Strip out mistaken interpretations, premature commitments, and narrow hypotheses that would anchor future turns. The goal is a clean restart without losing useful progress.\n\n#### 12) Distinguish right result from right approach\nA final answer can be acceptable even if intermediate reasoning wandered, and a polished method can still be wrong if it violated user requirements. Keep result alignment and method alignment separate in the analysis so a successful end state does not erase earlier mismatches.\n\n#### 13) Prefer forward-looking, corrective diagnoses\nThe most useful analysis is the one that helps the next response converge quickly. Phrase issues in a way that identifies the current gap between the accumulated user spec and the assistant\u2019s behavior, rather than narrating the whole conversation. Make the correction actionable without prescribing implementation details.\n\n#### 14) Use compact, transferable failure labels\nPrefer labels that generalize across domains: assumed unstated constraint, ignored newly revealed constraint, overcommitted before full disclosure, ignored earlier constraint still in force, or optimized for an early shard that was later superseded. These labels are more useful than domain-specific explanations and help the analyzer stay reusable.\n\n#### 15) Recheck validity under later-disclosed constraints\nIn shard-based conversations, earlier assistant work should be re-evaluated whenever new user details arrive. A response that was plausible at turn N may become incompatible at turn N+1. The analysis should explicitly note when earlier alignment no longer holds after disclosure updates.",
